Given,
$\begin{aligned}
i & =15 \mathrm{~A} \\
t & =10 \times 60=600 \mathrm{~s} \\
q & =\text { it }=15 \times 600=9000 \mathrm{C}
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& \text {Equivalent voltage }=\frac{\varepsilon_1 r_1+\varepsilon_2 r_2}{r_1+r_2} \\
& \qquad \begin{aligned}
& =\frac{1 \times 0.1+5 \times 0.1}{0.1+0.1} \\
\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{eq}} & =\frac{0.6}{0.2}=3 \mathrm{~V}
\end{aligned}
\end{aligned}$
From first circuit, we have
$i=\frac{\varepsilon}{\mathrm{R}+r}$
Value of $r$ is very less can be neglected as compared to R
Now,
$\begin{aligned}
i & =\frac{\varepsilon}{\mathrm{R}} \\
10^{-3} & =\frac{1}{\mathrm{R}} \\
\mathrm{R} & =10^3=1000 \Omega
\end{aligned}$
Now, from second circuit
$\begin{aligned}
i_1 & =\frac{\mathrm{E}_{\mathrm{eq}}}{\mathrm{R}+r_{e q}}=\frac{3}{1000} \\
i_1 & =3 \times 10^{-3} \\
& =3 \mathrm{~mA}
\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{K} \cdot \mathrm{E}=\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{mv}^2 \\
& \Rightarrow 100=\frac{1}{2} \times 2 \times \mathrm{V}^2 \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{~V}^2=100 \\
& \Rightarrow \mathrm{~V}=10 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s}
\end{aligned}$

Since the electron moves through a potential difference, the gain in kinetic energy will be equal to the Potential energy lost by the electron.
The energy can be given by the formula $\mathrm{E}=\mathrm{VQ}$, where ' E ' represents potential energy in joules, and ' Q ' represents charge in coulombs.
$\begin{aligned}
& \mathrm{E}=(1 \mathrm{kV})\left(1.6 \times 10^{-19} \mathrm{~J}\right) \\
& \mathrm{E}=1.6 \times 10^{-16} \mathrm{~J}
\end{aligned}$

The increase in the Volume of a body due to the increase in its temperature is called thermal expansion (Volume).
Coefficient of volume expansion is given by, $3 / 2 \times$ Coeff. of areal expansion
$\begin{aligned}
& =3 / 2 \times 1.6 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\
& =2.4 \times 10^{-5} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}
\end{aligned}$

